- op: discuss  # cd1d
  at: '2026-03-02T23:44:23Z'  # cd1d
  by: agent  # cd1d
  actor: jgstern_agent  # cd1d
  clock: 2  # cd1d
  nonce: cd1d  # cd1d
  message: "External bakeoff (March 2026) found the same class of issue in Vault: 107 of 130 unlinked route nodes are in ui/mirage/ — Ember.js Mirage test stubs that simulate server endpoints for frontend testing. These are test infrastructure but aren't in a standard test directory, so they're classified as production code, inflating the unlinked-route count from 75% to 25%.\n\nThis is the same pattern as src/dev/ in halo2: non-standard paths containing test/dev infrastructure get misclassified as production code. The fix for src/dev/ (skip dev match after source roots) won't cover ui/mirage. The deeper issue is that test-path detection relies on a fixed set of directory name patterns rather than understanding framework-specific test infrastructure locations.\n\nConsider whether the fix should be:\na) Narrow: add specific patterns (src/dev/, ui/mirage/) one at a time\nb) Structural: add a framework-aware test-directory detection hook that frameworks like Ember/Mirage can register (similar to how framework detection works for routes)\n\nSource: /home/jgstern_agent/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/hypergumbo-v2.1.0-bakeoff-jenkins-kong-vault-20260302.md"  # cd1d
- op: discuss  # d2ef
  at: '2026-03-03T06:16:50Z'  # d2ef
  by: agent  # d2ef
  actor: jgstern_agent  # d2ef
  clock: 3  # d2ef
  nonce: d2ef  # d2ef
  message: "Second external bakeoff (March 2026) found another manifestation: bun's 86 detected routes are 100% from test files (test/js/node/, test/js/third_party/). bun is a JavaScript runtime, not a web app — its \"routes\" are test fixtures that instantiate Express/Fastify/Node HTTP servers to test compatibility. Zero routes come from production code.\n\nThis is test infrastructure leaking into production analysis — same structural class as src/dev/ (halo2) and ui/mirage/ (Vault). The test-path detection catches standard test/ directories for exclusion purposes, but the route detector runs before test filtering, and route nodes created from test files aren't flagged as test-tier.\n\nAdditionally: grafana's top http_route entrypoint is a React component event handler (handleRemove in VariableStaticOptionsFormItems.tsx) classified as [http_route] HTTP DELETE /:uid at confidence 1.00. Frontend code in components/ directories is being misclassified as server-side route handlers. This is a different vector (framework detection false positive rather than test path misclassification) but the same downstream effect: noise in production analysis."  # d2ef
